1
00:00:00,850 --> 00:00:07,224
The brush clears a gold coin, stamped with a king in armour. It is a dinara of the Gupta age.

2
00:00:07,224 --> 00:00:14,644
Gold coins like this were issued in large numbers. The age of Chandragupta the Second is often called a golden age.

3
00:00:14,644 --> 00:00:18,390
How can iron stay bright for sixteen hundred years?

4
00:00:36,317 --> 00:00:45,540
How do we know? Gold dinaras of many kinds survive. Fa-Hien, a Chinese Buddhist monk, travelled in India for several years.

5
00:00:45,540 --> 00:00:50,127
The iron pillar at Mehrauli has an inscription naming a king Chandra.

6
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:15,097
Chandragupta the Second ruled about 375 to 415 CE. He took the title Vikramaditya, the sun of valour.

7
00:01:15,097 --> 00:01:23,877
He defeated the Shaka rulers of western India, and gained Malwa and Gujarat, with its ports. Ujjain became a second capital.

8
00:01:23,877 --> 00:01:31,993
He made marriage alliances too. His daughter Prabhavati Gupta married a Vakataka king, and ruled as regent after his death.

9
00:01:31,993 --> 00:01:41,557
Fa-Hien visited about 399 to 414 CE. He found the people prosperous and the roads safe, and he noted that punishments were mild.

10
00:01:41,557 --> 00:01:49,233
Tradition says that nine scholars, the Navaratnas, adorned his court. Kalidasa is the most famous name.

11
00:01:49,233 --> 00:01:51,310
Scholars debate this tradition.

12
00:01:51,310 --> 00:01:59,810
In Delhi, the Mehrauli iron pillar, which has not rusted, is linked to a king named Chandra, often identified with him.

13
00:02:16,217 --> 00:02:25,780
Old name, new name. The Chinese monk, once written as Fa-Hien, is now usually spelled Faxian in modern Chinese romanisation.

14
00:02:25,780 --> 00:02:32,154
The name means Splendour of the Law. He left a record called A Record of Buddhist Kingdoms.

15
00:02:48,583 --> 00:02:57,815
Carve this tip into memory. Vikramaditya: Malwa won. Fa-Hien watched. A title, a conquest and a witness.

16
00:02:57,815 --> 00:03:01,308
Those three are the core of Chandragupta the Second.

17
00:03:17,717 --> 00:03:33,046
Dig Quiz. Pause and name it. Number one. Name the Gupta gold coin. The answer: the dinara. Number two.

18
00:03:33,046 --> 00:03:49,507
Where in Delhi stands the famous iron pillar? The answer: Mehrauli. Number three. Which Chinese monk visited in his time?

19
00:03:49,507 --> 00:04:00,171
The answer: Fa-Hien. Now one line for Mains. Why is the age of the Guptas called a golden age? Is it justified?

20
00:04:20,583 --> 00:04:29,807
The gold coin goes into its case. It is a dinara of the Gupta age. Three facts to take away. Gupta gold dinara.

21
00:04:29,807 --> 00:04:41,426
Of Chandragupta II, about 375 to 415 CE. Fa-Hien visited in his reign. Next gallery: Gupta Society and Economy.

